{"name":"Under the Radar","hex":"#476e42","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"under the radar","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Prepositional phrase","definitions":["Without attracting notice; in an undetected or secretive manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/under_the_radar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Under the Radar (Daniel Powter album)","blocks":[{"text":"Under the Radar is the third studio album by Canadian singer-songwriter Daniel Powter and follows his 2005 self-titled second album. The album was released in September 2008 in Europe and was later released in North America in 2009. The song \"Next Plane Home\" was released as the first single of the album. It was followed by \"Best of Me\" and then \"Whole World Around\". This album also contains a new remix of \"Love You Lately\" which has a cleaner sound, extra keyboard/synthesizer effects, and some extra backing vocals as well as a re-recording of the song \"Negative Fashion\" which originally appeared on Powter's debut album \"I'm Your Betty\". In the UK, the album has debuted at number 43 and included an exclusive bonus track in the form of a live rendition of \"Bad Day\" in Vienna."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b8/Under_the_Radar_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Under_the_Radar_(Daniel_Powter_album)","wikidata":"Q7883366"},{"title":"Under the Radar (Dispatch album)","blocks":[{"text":"Under the Radar is a 2002 documentary DVD of the band Dispatch. It also includes a bonus CD entitled \"Patchwork\", which includes remixes of Dispatch songs and audio recordings of some of the performances on the DVD."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/1e/Dispatch-undertheradar.jpg/330px-Dispatch-undertheradar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Under_the_Radar_(Dispatch_album)","wikidata":"Q7883363"},{"title":"Under the Radar (magazine)","blocks":[{"text":"Under the Radar is an American music magazine that features interviews with accompanying photo-shoots. Each issue includes opinion and commentary of the indie music scene as well as reviews of books, DVDs, and albums. The magazine posts web-exclusive interviews and reviews on its website."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Under_the_Radar_(magazine)","wikidata":"Q7883372"},{"title":"Radar","blocks":[{"text":"Radar is a system that uses radio waves to determine the distance (ranging), direction, and radial velocity of objects relative to the site. It is a radiodetermination method used to detect and track aircraft, ships, spacecraft, guided missiles, motor vehicles, weather formations and terrain. The term RADAR was coined in 1940 by the United States Navy as an acronym for \"radio detection and ranging\". The term radar has since entered English and other languages as an anacronym, a common noun, losing all capitalization."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Radar_antenna.jpg/330px-Radar_antenna.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radar","wikidata":"Q47528"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Radar-hatzerim-1-1.jpg/500px-Radar-hatzerim-1-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Radar-hatzerim-1-1.jpg/1280px-Radar-hatzerim-1-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Radar-hatzerim-1-1.jpg/500px-Radar-hatzerim-1-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARadar-hatzerim-1-1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Early_radar_antenna_-_US_Naval_Research_Laboratory_Anacostia.jpg/500px-Early_radar_antenna_-_US_Naval_Research_Laboratory_Anacostia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Early_radar_antenna_-_US_Naval_Research_Laboratory_Anacostia.jpg/500px-Early_radar_antenna_-_US_Naval_Research_Laboratory_Anacostia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Experimental radar antenna, US Naval Research Laboratory, Anacostia, D. C., from the late 1930s (photo taken in 1945)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEarly_radar_antenna_-_US_Naval_Research_Laboratory_Anacostia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Watson_Radar.jpg/500px-Watson_Radar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Watson_Radar.jpg/1280px-Watson_Radar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Watson_Radar.jpg/500px-Watson_Radar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The first workable unit built by Robert Watson-Watt and his team","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWatson_Radar.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/Chain_home.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/Chain_home.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A Chain Home tower in Great Baddow, Essex, United Kingdom","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChain_home.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Watson_watt_02_fr.jpg/500px-Watson_watt_02_fr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumb